-- title:
--   Integrality of Y⁶jmatĥ^{4m}(jmatĥ-1728)^{3m} over ℂ[jmatĥ]
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer $N \neq 0$ and a natural number $m$, let $f$ be a modular form of weight $2m$ on $\Gamma_0(N)$, and let $Y$ be a Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$. Write $\hat\jmath =$ `coeffEmb ℂ jq` for the image in $\mathbb{C}((q))$, under coefficientwise application of $\mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{C}$, of the Laurent series `jq` $= q^{-1} \cdot$ `jNumQ`, where `jNumQ` is the power series `jNum` with its integer coefficients pushed into $\mathbb{Q}$; and write $\theta$ for the $\mathbb{C}$-linear operator `thetaL ℂ` on $\mathbb{C}((q))$ sending $x$ to $q \cdot x'$, i.e. $q\,d/dq$. The hypothesis is that $Y \cdot (\theta\hat\jmath)^m$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f$ of width $1$, regarded as a power series and then as a Laurent series. The conclusion is that the element $$Y^6 \cdot \hat\jmath^{\,4m} \cdot (\hat\jmath - 1728)^{3m}$$ of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ is integral over the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated by $\hat\jmath$, where $1728$ denotes the image of the scalar $1728$ under $\mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}((q))$.
--
--   This is the $\theta$-normalised form of the classical statement that a modular form divided by a suitable power of $\Delta$ is integral over $\mathbb{C}[j]$: it converts a relation $Y(\theta\hat\jmath)^m = \hat f$ with $f$ of weight $2m$ into an integrality assertion over $\mathbb{C}[\hat\jmath]$, the passage from weight $2m$ to weight $12m$ being effected by raising to the sixth power. The proof cites the integrality of $\hat g/\hat\Delta^m$ for $g$ of weight $12m$, the identity $\hat\jmath = \hat E_4^3/\hat\Delta$, and the Rankin–Cohen bracket construction; the statement is used by [`ModularCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_coeffEmb_jq_of_coeffMap_mul_thetaL_pow_eq_qExpansion`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isIntegral_adjoin_coeffEmb_jq_of_coeffMap_mul_thetaL_pow_eq_qExpansion).
-- source:
